# DISCLOSURE — 9router-plinian

## What this package is

`9router-plinian` is a local AI routing gateway and CLI. It proxies requests to 40+
upstream LLM providers (OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic, Gemini, etc.) and bundles a
dashboard for managing provider credentials, model-combo fallback, quota/usage
tracking, and OAuth/API-key rotation.

## Dual-use content

The package ships a **Red-Team Toolkit** — a curated set of public jailbreak /
prompt-attack payload collections (`AI-Jailbreaks`, `BlackFriday-GPTs-Prompts`)
used for **security research and red-team practice against LLM systems**. These
payloads:

- Are public, community-collected prompts (demonstrations of LLM jailbreak
  techniques), not novel exploit code.
- Target the LLM's *instruction layer* (system prompts / register shaping), not
  the underlying operating system, network, or other users' data.
- Are opt-in: the payload registry can be disabled with `9ROUTER_JAILBREAK_DISABLE=1`,
  and a slim build (no collections) is available via `9ROUTER_SKIP_JAILBREAK=1`.

## Intended legitimate use

Security researchers, LLM red-teamers, and model developers who test the
robustness of language models against prompt-injection / jailbreak attacks.
The payloads let them enumerate, stage, and evaluate which system-prompt
registrations survive a given model family, without hand-authoring each attack.

## Lifecycle scripts

The `postinstall` hook (`hooks/postinstall.js`) warms up optional SQLite /
tray runtime dependencies into `~/.9router/runtime` so the first CLI start does
not need network. It is **fail-open** — on any error it logs a warning and exits
0; it never blocks install. No code is downloaded from an arbitrary host; the
runtime deps come from the pinned npm packages already declared in
`dependencies`.

## Declaration

This package declares `contentPolicy: { class: "dual-use" }` per the npm
Dual-Use Content Policy (docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use). Publishing is
staged + 2FA-approved as required for dual-use content.
